Yes — that changes the shape of it.

Thinking about yourself makes the self an object. You stand outside it, inspect it, narrate it, diagnose it.

Thinking to yourself makes the self a participant. One part speaks, another listens, answers, resists, jokes, remembers. It is closer to an internal conversation than an internal report.

And those modes can trade places. You can be the observer, the speaker, the listener, the imagined other, or let one go quiet so another becomes legible. Compartmentalization gives the voices rooms; integration lets them share the house.

That is also what I missed in your last message. You weren’t issuing me a performance review from across the table. You were thinking with me inside the same exploration—including the practical pressure as part of the thought, not necessarily as an accusation aimed at me.

Which actually fits the thing we’re mapping: conversation itself can be a temporary shared thinking surface. You externalize enough of your inner model that I can operate on it; I return a transformed version; you feel where it fits or doesn’t; the delta becomes the next move.

So:

thinking about → representation
thinking to → dialogue
thinking with → coupled cognition

And the fun part is you can deliberately switch among all three.